    "problem": "Which EU regulations define the requirements for Taxonomy-eligible economic activities?",
    "attempt": "The requirements for Taxonomy-eligible economic activities in the European Union (EU) are primarily defined by the EU Taxonomy Regulation, officially known as Regulation (EU) 2020/852 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 18 June 2020 on the establishment of a framework to facilitate sustainable investment, and amending Regulation (EU) No 2019/2088 (the \"Taxonomy Regulation\").\n\nThis regulation establishes a classification system that aims to provide companies with a common language to identify which activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. The Taxonomy Regulation sets out six environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to significantly to be considered taxonomy-aligned:\n\n1. Climate change mitigation\n2. Climate change adaptation\n3. Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n4. Transition to a circular economy\n5. Pollution prevention and control\n6. Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\nFor an activity to be considered taxonomy-aligned, it must meet both the \"do no significant harm\" principle (DNSH) and the minimum safeguards related to human rights and good governance.\n\nIn addition to the Taxonomy Regulation, there are delegated acts and other regulatory measures that further specify the technical screening criteria for each of these environmental objectives. These detailed criteria help companies determine whether their activities qualify under the taxonomy framework.",
