    "problem": "Which environmental objectives and their corresponding codes are defined under the EU Taxonomy in the context?",
    "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language for investors and companies to understand which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It defines six specific environmental objectives, each with its own code. Here they are:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation (TClMi)**: This objective focuses on reducing greenhouse gas emissions and enhancing carbon sinks.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation (TClAd)**: This objective aims at increasing resilience to the actual and expected impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources (TWaMa)**: This objective targets the sustainable use and protection of water resources and marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy (TCirE)**: This objective promotes the transition towards a circular economy, including waste prevention, re-use, recycling, and other forms of resource efficiency.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control (TPreC)**: This objective seeks to prevent or reduce pollution and control the release of harmful substances into the environment.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems (TBioE)**: This objective aims to protect and restore biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nEach of these objectives has detailed criteria and thresholds that must be met for an activity to be considered environmentally sustainable under the EU Taxonomy. These criteria help ensure that investments contribute positively to the environment while not causing significant harm to any of the other five objectives.",
