    "problem": "How does Zalando's reporting of capex related to the leasing of buildings demonstrate compliance with economic activity 7.7 under the Climate Delegated Act, and why is this activity significant for EU Taxonomy alignment?",
    "attempt": "Zalando, as a major online fashion retailer, needs to ensure that its capital expenditures (CapEx) related to the leasing of buildings comply with specific criteria set out in the EU Taxonomy Regulation, particularly under economic activity 7.7, which pertains to energy-efficient buildings. The Climate Delegated Act provides detailed criteria for how companies can report their activities to demonstrate alignment with the EU Taxonomy, which aims to provide a common framework for sustainable investment.\n\n### Compliance with Economic Activity 7.7\n\nEconomic activity 7.7 focuses on the construction or deep renovation of buildings to achieve high energy performance. For Zalando, this would include any new buildings it constructs or leases, as well as significant renovations of existing buildings, provided these actions meet certain thresholds for energy efficiency.\n\n#### Key Criteria:\n1. **Energy Performance**: Buildings must meet a minimum energy performance standard, typically defined as being at least 20% more efficient than the applicable national minimum energy performance standards.\n   \n2. **Renovation Thresholds**: For renovations, the project must result in a reduction of at least 30% in final energy consumption compared to the building’s pre-renovation state.\n\n3. **Lifecycle Assessment**: The overall lifecycle environmental impact of the building must be assessed, ensuring that the materials used and the construction process itself are environmentally sound.\n\n4. **Duration of Use**: The building must have a minimum expected duration of use of 50 years.\n\n### Reporting Requirements\n\nTo demonstrate compliance with economic activity 7.7, Zalando would need to report on several aspects:\n\n- **Technical Documentation**: Detailed documentation showing how the building meets the energy performance criteria.\n- **Lifecycle Analysis**: An analysis of the environmental impact over the building’s lifecycle, including material sourcing, construction processes, and operational energy use.\n- **Certification**: Where available, certification from recognized bodies that the building meets the required energy efficiency standards.\n- **Financial Information**: Details on the CapEx invested in the building, including how much was allocated to achieving the necessary energy efficiency levels.\n\n### Significance for EU Taxonomy Alignment\n\nThe significance of aligning with economic activity 7.7 lies in several key areas:\n\n1. **Sustainable Investment**: By demonstrating compliance, Zalando can attract investors who are looking to support sustainable projects, thereby potentially securing more favorable financing terms.\n\n2. **Regulatory Compliance**: Aligning with the EU Taxonomy helps Zalando comply with evolving regulatory requirements around sustainability, reducing the risk of non-compliance penalties.\n\n3. **Brand Reputation**: Demonstrating commitment to sustainability can enhance Zalando’s brand reputation among consumers and stakeholders who prioritize environmental responsibility.\n\n4. **Operational Efficiency**: Investing in energy-efficient buildings can lead to long-term cost savings through reduced energy bills and improved operational efficiency.\n\nIn summary, Zalando’s reporting on CapEx related to the leasing of buildings must clearly show how these investments contribute to higher energy efficiency standards, thus aligning with economic activity 7.7 of the EU Taxonomy. This not only ensures compliance with regulatory requirements but also supports broader sustainability goals and enhances the company’s position in the market.",
