    "id": "train:005956:qa_pairs_non_factoid:0011",
    "problem": "What specific aspects of the EU Taxonomy regulatory framework define the scope of assurance procedures performed on the Non-Financial Report, and why are other elements of the sustainability report excluded?",
    "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide a common language for companies to disclose how their economic activities contribute to environmental sustainability. It defines six environmental objectives: climate change mitigation, climate change adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nWhen it comes to the scope of assurance procedures performed on the Non-Financial Report (which includes information related to the EU Taxonomy), several key aspects of the EU Taxonomy framework influence what is included:\n\n1. **Alignment with Environmental Objectives**: Assurance procedures focus on verifying whether the reported activities align with the six environmental objectives defined in the EU Taxonomy. This means that only those activities that contribute significantly to one or more of these objectives are within the scope of assurance.\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) Principle**: The DNSH principle requires that activities contributing to one objective do not cause significant harm to any of the other five objectives. Assurance procedures will also check compliance with this principle.\n\n3. **Substantive Contribution**: Activities must make a substantive contribution to the relevant environmental objective. Assurance procedures will assess whether the reported activities meet this criterion.\n\n4. **Minimum Safeguards**: Companies must comply with minimum safeguards, which include adherence to social and labor rights, human rights, and good governance standards. While these are important, they are not part of the core taxonomy objectives and thus may be excluded from the primary scope of assurance focused on the taxonomy.\n\nOther elements of the sustainability report are often excluded from the specific scope of EU Taxonomy-related assurance procedures because:\n\n- **Non-Relevance to Environmental Objectives**: Elements that do not directly relate to the six environmental objectives defined by the EU Taxonomy are typically outside the scope. For example, information about corporate governance, employee diversity, or community engagement, while crucial for overall sustainability reporting, do not fall under the specific criteria of the EU Taxonomy.\n  \n- **Focus on Specificity**: The assurance process is designed to be highly targeted towards the specific criteria laid out in the EU Taxonomy. This specificity ensures that the assurance provided is meaningful and directly addresses the environmental impact of the company's activities.\n\n- **Regulatory Compliance**: The EU Taxonomy is a regulatory framework aimed at standardizing disclosures related to environmental sustainability. Other elements of the sustainability report, while important, may be governed by different regulations or frameworks, such as the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD) or the upcoming Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD).\n\nIn summary, the scope of assurance procedures on the Non-Financial Report, particularly regarding the EU Taxonomy, is narrowly defined to ensure alignment with specific environmental objectives, compliance with the DNSH principle, and a substantive contribution to these objectives. Other elements of the sustainability report are excluded to maintain the focus and relevance of the assurance process to the core environmental goals of the EU Taxonomy.",
    "solution": "The assurance procedures were performed solely on the Non-Financial Report (NfR) of Hapag-Lloyd AG to assess its compliance with specified regulatory requirements, excluding other information in the sustainability report or GRI standards.",
