    "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system designed to provide a common language for investors and companies to understand which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It includes detailed technical screening criteria that economic activities must meet to be classified as contributing substantially to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the taxonomy.\n\nFor electric vehicles (EVs) to meet the EU Taxonomy's criteria, they must not only be zero-emission but also comply with additional sustainability requirements throughout their lifecycle, including production, use, and end-of-life disposal. These criteria cover aspects such as greenhouse gas emissions, air pollution, water use, and waste management.\n\nOne specific area where tyre requirements come into play is related to the overall environmental impact of the vehicle. Tyres contribute significantly to a vehicle's total environmental footprint due to factors like rolling resistance, wear and tear, and the materials used in their manufacture. The EU Taxonomy may consider these factors when assessing whether an EV meets its sustainability criteria.\n\nHere are some ways tyre requirements might affect an EV's compliance with the EU Taxonomy:\n\n1. **Rolling Resistance**: Lower rolling resistance can improve fuel efficiency and reduce energy consumption, thereby lowering CO2 emissions. However, achieving low rolling resistance while maintaining safety standards can be challenging.\n\n2. **Tyre Composition**: The materials used in tyres can have varying environmental impacts. For instance, the use of recycled materials versus virgin materials, and the potential for toxic substances in tyre composition, are all considerations.\n\n3. **Wear and Tear**: The rate at which tyres wear down can affect the frequency of replacement, which in turn affects resource consumption and waste generation.\n\n4. **End-of-Life Management**: Proper recycling and disposal of 